MOD_HTTP3 VERSIONING
====================


INTRODUCTION
------------
mod_http3 is an Apache httpd module that adds HTTP/3 support over QUIC.
This document describes how the project uses version numbers, how
releases are classified, and how compatibility guarantees evolve as the
project matures toward its first stable release.

The project follows the general versioning philosophy of the Apache HTTP
Server, adapted to the realities of a young, rapidly evolving module.


VERSION NUMBERING
-----------------
Versions follow the pattern MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH:

  * MAJOR -- incremented for incompatible changes that affect the public
    API surface or require significant migration effort.
  * MINOR -- even numbers (0, 2, 4, …) denote stable/GA branches; odd
    numbers (1, 3, 5, …) denote development/alpha/beta branches.
  * PATCH -- incremented for each release within a minor branch.

The version is defined in one authoritative location:

    project(mod_http3 VERSION X.Y.Z)         # CMakeLists.txt (line 3)

CMake generates mod_http3/include/h3_version.h from this value,
providing compile-time constants:

    MOD_HTTP3_VERSION_MAJOR    — major component
    MOD_HTTP3_VERSION_MINOR    — minor component
    MOD_HTTP3_VERSION_PATCH    — patch component
    MOD_HTTP3_VERSION          — packed 24-bit integer (0xMMmmpp)
    MOD_HTTP3_VERSION_STRING   — human-readable "X.Y.Z"


PRE-1.0: EXPERIMENTAL PHASE (current)
--------------------------------------
The project is currently in the 0.0.x series. During this phase:

  * The module carries an "experimental" status.
  * The configuration directive interface may change between patch 
    releases.
  * The internal C API is not yet stable and may be reorganised.
  * Each 0.0.x release is a self-contained snapshot; there are no
    back-port branches.
  * Binary compatibility between releases is not guaranteed; always
    rebuild after upgrading.

The 0.0.x series is expected to transition to 0.1.x (development) and
then to 0.2.x (first stable) or directly to 1.0.0, depending on the
scope of remaining API changes.


STABLE RELEASES, X.{even}.Z
----------------------------
Once the project reaches its first stable release, even-numbered minor
versions will carry these guarantees for the lifetime of the minor
branch:

  * The httpd directive interface (H3* directives) is forward compatible.
    New directives may be added; existing directives will not be removed
    or have their semantics changed incompatibly.

  * The CMake build interface (WITH_*, BUILD_*, ENABLE_* options) is
    forward compatible. Options that work in one patch release will
    continue to work in the next.

  * Module output (mod_http3.so) built against one patch release will
    load into the same httpd used to build any other patch release
    within the same minor branch (same httpd MMN).

  * No experimental features are introduced directly into a stable
    branch. New features are developed in the corresponding odd-minor
    development branch and promoted to stable only after they are proven
    and compatible.


DEVELOPMENT RELEASES, X.{odd}.Z
---------------------------------
Odd-numbered minor versions are the active development branches, always
one greater than the current stable minor:

  * API and directive changes are permitted at any time.
  * Internal source reorganisation may occur between patch releases.
  * Binary compatibility across development patch releases is not
    guaranteed.
  * Development releases may be classified as alpha, beta, or GA to
    reflect the perceived stability of the tree.
  * Any committer may cut a development release at any time.

Features that prove stable in the development branch may be promoted to
the next stable release by a consensus vote of active committers. A
feature cannot change once promoted, so the vote must confirm that the
interface and behaviour are final.


RELEASE CANDIDATES
------------------
To avoid "skipped" version numbers, every release is first tagged as a
release candidate:

    vX.Y.Z-rc1, vX.Y.Z-rc2, …

Pushing the candidate tag is what publishes it: the release workflow
builds and hashes the artifacts with scripts/release.sh and uploads them
to a GitHub draft prerelease. A candidate is promoted to the final
vX.Y.Z tag only after it receives at least three +1 votes from active
PMC members with no unresolved issues.

Note that artifacts are named from the CMake project version, so an
-rcN candidate and the final release produce identically named files.
The candidate is identified by its tag and its GitHub prerelease, not
by the artifact filenames.

If a candidate fails its vote, the tag is removed, the issue is fixed,
and a new candidate suffix is used (rc2, rc3, …).

See docs/release-process.md for the complete release engineering
workflow.


MAJOR VERSION BUMPS
-------------------
When promoting a development branch to stable, the committers decide
whether the accumulated changes warrant a major version bump. The
rule of thumb: if porting user configurations or rebuilding with the
new version requires non-trivial effort, the major version should be
incremented.


CHANGELOG
---------
All user-visible changes are recorded in the CHANGES file. Entries are
listed in reverse chronological order. Security-related entries always
appear at the top of their release block, prefixed with "SECURITY:".
